WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.190
Good morning John and Hank, it's Friday.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.720
My name is Rosianna. I'm John's producing

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.730
partner, John and Hanks's secret sister, a youtuber

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.620
over at youtube.com/Rosianna and an

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.240
avid user of the internet. That being

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.340
said, today's video is about logging off.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.380
Not too long ago, I hit my limit of the

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.450
social internet, I scrambled my passwords,

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.160
I deleted apps from my phone, I backed

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.840
quickly away and promptly read more

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.980
books in a week than I'd read in the

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.420
previous two months. I found that I was

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.949
less anxious, less angry and I

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:24.539
communicated far better with friends

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:26.369
about how I was actually doing. I'm not here

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:28.380
to say, though, that the internet is bad. For one,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.789
I live thousands of kilometers away from

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:31.320
most of my friends so I love seeing

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.300
updates from Nando's or from the central

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.950
line. For two, I don't think the Internet

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.719
is intrinsically anything. It's a tool

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:38.399
and system we use, and I read a piece in

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:40.290
Offscreen magazine recently that kind

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.370
of summed this up for me.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.809
We think the Internet is inherently a

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.670
certain way but it isn't. It's constantly

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:45.960
changing, and we are the ones who are

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:46.680
changing it.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.030
We can change it for the better, rather

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.680
than having to change ourselves to adapt

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.539
the parts that are toxic for us.

00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:53.219
The internet seems like it's made up of code

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.690
and clouds, but really it's made up of

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.520
people. Like so much, it comes back to

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.199
community. Prior to my mini internet

00:00:58.199 --> 00:00:59.820
break, part my reluctance to step back

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.800
is that I've defined myself through the internet.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.329
I've defended the internet to other people.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.680
I've defended the joy of meeting

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.390
strangers and the special thrill of

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.650
making something with people you will

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.970
never meet. Whether that's something

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.430
deeply useful, deeply silly, or something

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.689
that fall somewhere between the two that

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:13.979
may bring a smile, but won't necessarily

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:15.720
feed the world. A friend of mine

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.100
suggested to me that maybe the murkiness

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.720
of the internet, that led to me taking a

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.310
break, and is infused to all of these

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.960
discussions of online discourse, is that

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.790
it wasn't really designed to hold so

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.259
many people. Maybe when I used to

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:26.939
log on to AOL, always after 6pm and

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:28.530
only if my sisters weren't on the phone,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.390
there were fewer people online and that's what

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.079
made it fun.

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:32.430
We were working it out together and building

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.320
it together, rather than entering an internet

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.909
which felt as ready-made as Ikea

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:37.950
furniture. With experts and prose and

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.930
rights and wrongs, and yes, the occasional

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.579
missing bolt. I struggle to believe that,

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:43.049
though, I think we're still building the

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:44.610
internet, there are still voices we aren't

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.439
hearing, there is still work to do.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:47.640
It reminds me of one of my favourite scenes

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.079
from The West Wing, when a character called

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:50.880
Jeff asked Josh to take out a dollar

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.890
bill, turn it over and look at the

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.840
incomplete pyramid. He says the seal

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.280
is meant to be unfinished because this

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.960
country's meant to be unfinished. We're

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.729
meant to keep doing better. Yes, it's

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:00.730
idealistic, but I

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.260
genuinely believe that. The internet is

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.570
not an iron bunker, buried deep deep deep deep

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.790
underground, it's infrastructure. And as

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.470
humans change, and we learn more about

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.570
what helps us and helps other people,

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.040
the internet will change, and we will

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.480
change it. We will have more and better

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.310
ideas about what to do next,

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.170
not what to do last. Having said all of

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.670
that with such confidence, I think we are

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.170
at a time of the internet when a lot of people

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.730
claim to know the right answer. And the

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.380
idea of the mass of humanity can be

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.090
broken down into bullet point lists or

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:27.040
alphas and betas is to me contradictory

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.480
to everything that humans have

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.370
demonstrated over time. Maybe we start by

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.230
not knowing, and what was helpful to me in my

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.330
time stepping back from the internet was

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.130
realizing how bloody long it had been

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.560
since I had clicked logout or log off;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.420
since I had made an intentional and

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.490
active decision about my online behaviour,

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.320
rather than a passive one. It clicked though

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.610
when I open my computer, I'm often

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.140
on automatic: my twitter feed, my inbox, my calendar.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.680
I'm kind of in a nothing state, and

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:51.370
in that nothing state, I am plasticine,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.470
and who am I handing that plasticine to?

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.330
Just to get fully quote-tastic here, in a

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.860
Lenny Letter, Zadie Smith recently

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.360
wrote: "The feels have to possess a

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.070
certain amount of vertical depth. It's

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.690
like lowering a stone down into the well

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.400
of yourself, and the further it goes the

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.200
deeper it resounds. A lot of the social

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.760
platforms provoke feelings in me I

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.380
simply don't enjoy. For a moment, I am

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.420
flattered, falsely puffed up, briefly

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.820
amused, painfully hurt or infuriated."

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.260
John, you recommended to me the brilliant

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.940
podcast 'Invisibilia', and on that there

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.620
was an episode about how, in Rwanda,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.390
legislation had been passed to require

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.040
equal representation in government. But

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.360
that episode was also about how that

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.610
legislation didn't necessarily affect or

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.410
improve gender inequality around the

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:27.910
country. Essentially, and I'll put the full

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:29.320
episode in the description bar below because

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.880
it's great, that shortcut didn't quite

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.290
work and it may be wonder whether

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.730
Twitter was a shortcut that didn't quite

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.530
work, whether we needed to do more long

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.480
hard work to get to some of the benefits

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.280
and include some of the benefits that

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.260
website like Twitter provide: hearing from

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.330
more voices, community organizing. Because

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.770
otherwise we maybe have a bit of a

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.020
tendency to feel stuck or feel like

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.550
those are the ultimate solutions.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.200
The long hard work maybe doesn't let us

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.120
pretend to rest easy and admire the

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.400
pyramid and see it as complete. The truth

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.140
is I don't know, and I'm curious to hear

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.040
your thoughts.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.570
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

